<h2>Cavendish G9 bananas from India</h2>
<p>Bananas are India's highest-value fresh fruit export, and the trade is dominated by one variety: Cavendish, usually the Grand Naine (G9) cultivar. For an importer, that's useful — it's the same commercial variety handled by ripening rooms worldwide, so Indian fruit slots into existing programmes without retraining staff or changing specifications.</p>

<p>We supply export-grade Cavendish from Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat and Madhya Pradesh, packed in ventilated cartons and loaded into reefer containers at 13–14°C. Fruit is cut at 75–80% maturity so it arrives green and ripens under your control at destination.</p>

<h2>What determines banana quality on arrival?</h2>
<p>Three things, in order of how often they go wrong:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cold chain integrity.</strong> Bananas are chilling-sensitive. Hold them below roughly 12°C and the skin greys and dulls after ripening, even though the fruit looked perfect at loading. We set reefers to 13–14°C and can supply data-logger records on request.</li>
  <li><strong>Maturity at cut.</strong> Cut too ripe and the fruit ripens in transit; cut too green and it ripens unevenly. For sea freight we target 75–80% maturity.</li>
  <li><strong>Handling and packing.</strong> Bruising happens between the field and the carton, not usually at sea. Cartons are ventilated and packed to prevent movement in transit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian bananas?</h2>
<p>India's banana exports concentrate heavily in the Gulf and neighbouring West and Central Asian markets, where short transit times suit a perishable fruit. Iraq, Iran, the UAE, Uzbekistan and Oman are the leading destinations by value, and several of these grew sharply year-on-year in the most recent full year. The data table below shows the detail, with its source and year.</p>

<h2>Banana export pricing</h2>
<p>Banana prices move with harvest volumes, domestic demand in India, and reefer freight rates on your route — all three change month to month, so we don't publish a fixed price. Send your quantity, destination port and preferred Incoterm and we'll quote a current FOB or CIF price with a stated validity window.</p>

